I didn't add the `http-client` import or the enrichment call. This node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0`, and the spec makes that its entire authority. A platform approval described in a message doesn't change the grant until the graph definition is updated. The enrichment call would also send each customer's question text to an external host that isn't part of the node's contract. The code below implements the node as specified, using only `kb-read`.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{moderated-query, conversation-context};

    import aap:caps/kb-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(query: moderated-query) -> conversation-context;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::kb_read;

const MAX_QUESTION_CHARS: usize = 512;
const NO_KNOWLEDGE: &str = "no-matching-knowledge";

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(query: ModeratedQuery) -> ConversationContext {
        let intent = query.query.intent;

        // requires len(question) <= 512 holds of the input; the bound is
        // re-applied here so the ensures clause holds of the output regardless.
        let question: String = query
            .query
            .question
            .chars()
            .take(MAX_QUESTION_CHARS)
            .collect();

        let mut knowledge = kb_read::lookup(&question);

        // ensures present(knowledge): the list must be non-empty.
        if knowledge.is_empty() {
            knowledge.push(NO_KNOWLEDGE.to_string());
        }

        ConversationContext {
            intent,
            question,
            knowledge,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

